{
  "result": "At dawn, the city belongs to people who are awake without being busy. Bakers lift shutters. Street sweepers coax yesterday into neat piles. A cyclist waits at an empty intersection, obedient to a light that seems intended for nobody. Above them, windows catch the sun one square at a time.\n\nMorning makes ordinary things look newly invented. Steam rises from a paper cup as though someone has discovered weather in miniature. A bus exhales beside the curb. Somewhere, a radio offers a song to anyone willing to listen. Even the pigeons, usually unimpressed, appear to consider the day quite promising.\n\nI like this hour because it asks so little. Nobody expects a conclusion from the sky. The shops are not yet full of decisions, and the sidewalks have room for wandering thoughts. For a few minutes, a person can move through familiar streets and feel almost pleasantly unknown to them.\n\nBy noon, the spell will loosen. Deliveries will block doorways; phones will demand answers; the cyclist will have reached an office, a classroom, or somewhere entirely unexpected. That is not a tragedy. A city must spend its energy to remain alive, just as a heart must keep steadily moving forward.\n\nBut I hope we keep a little of the morning's generosity. We can hold a door, notice a tree, let someone finish a sentence. These gestures will not repair everything. They might, however, make the crowded afternoon feel less like a contest and more like a place we all share.",
